     1	# Medicare drug price negotiation: what is known, what others have written, and how they used our data
     2	
     3	Literature and grey-literature scan for the IRA negotiation study ([pharma/ira_findings.md](../pharma/ira_findings.md)). Searched 2026-10-20.
     4	
     5	**How this was searched.** Three parallel search legs (Claude Opus 5.5 subagents): peer-reviewed and working papers (OpenAlex, PubMed, PMC full text, NBER/SSRN), grey literature (CMS, CBO, MedPAC, KFF, consultancies, pharmacy associations, trade press, legal trackers), and prior uses of our data sources (NADAC, Part D Spending by Drug, the MFP file, SDUD). Their full tables, with about 180 entries, are in [ira_notes/academic.md](ira_notes/academic.md), [ira_notes/grey.md](ira_notes/grey.md) and [ira_notes/data_uses.md](ira_notes/data_uses.md). Verification: every one of the 108 DOIs cited across the three files resolves (107 in Crossref, one Zenodo DataCite DOI opened directly), and the DOI titles of the papers used below match their descriptions. The grey-literature claims this summary relies on were reopened at the source (CMS MTF fact sheet, MedPAC March 2026 chapter 13, NCPA's WAC-decrease alert and refund-delay notice, Drug Channels, 46brooklyn, the Supreme Court denial); figures that could not be confirmed at the source are left out or marked. Claims about papers come from abstracts unless marked full text; two papers' numbers come only from press coverage and are marked (press). The Consensus quota was exhausted and OpenAlex hit its daily limit near the end, so a few searches (commercial-price spillovers, inflation-rebate studies, stock-market event studies) did not run. This is a scoping scan, not a systematic review.
     6	
     7	---
     8	
     9	## 1. The program in one page
    10	
    11	- **Law and timeline.** The Inflation Reduction Act (Pub. L. 117-169, August 2022) lets Medicare negotiate prices (Maximum Fair Prices, MFPs) for high-spend single-source drugs: small molecules after 7 years on the market (9 years before prices apply), biologics after 11 (13). First 10 Part D drugs selected 2023-08-29, prices published 2024-08-15, **in effect 2026-01-01**. Second cohort of 15 Part D drugs: prices published 2025-11-25, effective 2027-01-01. Third cohort of 15 (first Part B drugs, plus Tradjenta renegotiated): selected 2026-01-27; final offers due 2026-09-30, acceptance by 2026-10-31, **prices published by 2026-11-30**, effective 2028-01-01. Full verified timeline in [grey.md](ira_notes/grey.md).
    12	- **What CMS claims.** Against 2023 prices, the first 10 MFPs would have saved 22% of net spending (about $6 billion); the 2027 cohort 44% (about $12 billion; 36% or $8.5 billion counting coverage-gap discounts). Wouters et al. 2024 (JAMA) argue the 22% overstates savings because the comparison is not to current net prices.
    13	- **The list is already shrinking.** Stelara, Entresto and Xarelto (generic or biosimilar entry, determined October 2025) and NovoLog/Fiasp (March 2026) leave the program on 2027-01-01; Xeljanz (2028 cohort) on 2029-01-01.
    14	- **Legal status.** The Supreme Court declined to hear the manufacturers' challenges on 2026-05-18 (AstraZeneca, Novo Nordisk, Novartis, Bristol Myers Squibb, Janssen, Boehringer Ingelheim; [Duane Morris](https://www.duanemorris.com/alerts/supreme_court_declines_hear_challenges_inflation_reduction_act_medicare_drug_price_0526.html)). The D.C. Circuit ruled for CMS in Teva's case on 2026-08-18; AbbVie's Botox suit (February 2026) is pending.
    15	- **Political changes around it.** The 2025 reconciliation law broadened the orphan-drug exclusion from the 2028 cohort (CBO: $8.8 billion over 10 years, via secondary reporting). The administration's most-favored-nation deals set a $245 Medicare GLP-1 price, below the $274 semaglutide MFP, with a GLP-1 bridge outside Part D through 2027; the GLOBE Part B model exempts drugs with an MFP in effect. The "pill penalty" fix (EPIC Act) was not found to have passed. A proposed rule (CMS-4215-P, 2026-06-16) would codify the program.
    16	
    17	## 2. How big are the price cuts, and against what
    18	
    19	The single most important lesson for our study: **list, net and MFP are three different prices, and these drugs carried very large rebates.**
    20	
    21	| Source | What it shows |
    22	|---|---|
    23	| MedPAC, March 2026, ch. 13 ([PDF](https://www.medpac.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Mar26_Ch13_MedPAC_Report_To_Congress_SEC.pdf), read in full) | In 2024, rebates reduced gross Part D costs by **nearly 45% for the 2026 cohort** and about 34% for the 2027 cohort (all single-source drugs: just under 30%). Rebates on the 2026 drugs totalled nearly $28 billion, about 35% of all Part D rebates. MedPAC concludes the program looks more effective for the 2027 cohort, which had less rebate competition. |
    24	| Wouters et al. 2024, JAMA ([10.1001/jama.2024.22582](https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2024.22582)) | IPAY 2026 MFPs were 8% (dapagliflozin) to 42% (sitagliptin) below estimated 2021 net prices, and 1.1 to 4.3 times prices in six other high-income countries. |
    25	| Hernandez, Gabriel & Dickson 2023, JMCP ([10.18553/jmcp.2023.29.8.868](https://doi.org/10.18553/jmcp.2023.29.8.868)); Sullivan et al. 2024, Value in Health ([10.1016/j.jval.2024.08.001](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2024.08.001)) | Before prices were announced: the statutory ceiling bound for only a few drugs (etanercept, ibrutinib, ustekinumab); for others, existing net prices were already below the ceiling. |
    26	| Jeon et al. 2026, Value in Health ([10.1016/j.jval.2026.03.010](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2026.03.010)) | Projected 2026 Part D **net** spending on the 10 drugs: $26.1 billion without negotiation vs $23.8 billion with it, **−8.8%**. |
    27	| Gabriel et al. 2026, JMCP ([10.18553/jmcp.2026.32.5.530](https://doi.org/10.18553/jmcp.2026.32.5.530)); Li et al. 2026, Value in Health ([10.1016/j.jval.2025.09.3054](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jval.2025.09.3054)) | IPAY 2027 MFPs: a median 38% below estimated net prices (range 15–60%), yet a median 108% above purchasing-power-adjusted prices in most-favored-nation countries. Li et al. had predicted 32–78% off list. |
    28	
    29	**What this means for our finding 1.** Our −64% median (−61% fixed-mix) drop in gross Part D spending per claim is mostly the **list price collapsing onto something close to the old net price**. With 45% rebates on these drugs, a 60% gross drop is consistent with a net saving in the single digits to low teens, which is what Jeon et al. project. Our headline must keep "gross" in the first sentence and set it beside the net projections.
    30	
    31	## 3. What has been observed since 2026-01-01
    32	
    33	**Realized spending: nothing published.** None of the three legs found a peer-reviewed paper, working paper or grey-literature report that measures realized 2026 Part D spending or prices for the ten drugs. Everything published is either a projection or a different outcome:
    34	
    35	| Outcome | Evidence |
    36	|---|---|
    37	| Formulary access | Jain & McCoy 2026, Health Affairs Scholar ([10.1093/haschl/qxag290](https://doi.org/10.1093/haschl/qxag290)): every plan covers all 10 drugs in 2026; step therapy on negotiated diabetes drugs rose to 5.0% (comparators under 1%). Zheng et al. 2026 ([10.1093/haschl/qxag171](https://doi.org/10.1093/haschl/qxag171)): access to non-selected brands in competitive classes fell 11.8 points in stand-alone plans, 2024–2026. |
    38	| Patient out-of-pocket | Castle et al. 2026, Annals ([10.7326/annals-26-02703](https://doi.org/10.7326/annals-26-02703)) (press): projected savings of about $685 a year (stand-alone plans) and $470 (Medicare Advantage); 26–49% lower OOP for six drugs, none for Stelara, Imbruvica and Enbrel users already at the $2,100 cap. Lederman et al. 2025, NBER ([10.3386/w33889](https://doi.org/10.3386/w33889)): with the 2025 cap and nonlinear cost sharing, most users see no change or a higher marginal OOP. |
    39	| Benefit redesign (confounder) | Anderson et al. 2026, Health Affairs ([10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00644](https://doi.org/10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00644)); Zhang et al. 2026, AJHP ([10.1093/ajhp/zxag083](https://doi.org/10.1093/ajhp/zxag083)): the 2025 redesign raised deductibles and shifted tiers to coinsurance. |
    40	| Pharmacy refunds | CMS fact sheet (April 2026, [PDF](https://www.cms.gov/files/document/pharmacy-dispensing-entity-mtf-fact-sheet.pdf), read in full): plans have 7 days to submit claim data and manufacturers 14 days to process; refunds reach pharmacies **within 21 days of the date of service, on average**. NCPA (2026-02-27, [notice](https://www.ncpa.org/newsroom/qam/2026/02/27/ncpa-shares-survey-results-cms-showing-mfp-refund-delays)): most member pharmacies waited 22 days or more and about a fifth more than 28 days; a majority drew on savings. (Exact survey percentages and sample size reported elsewhere could not be confirmed at the source.) Long-term-care pharmacies: Bhardwaj et al. 2026, JMCP ([10.18553/jmcp.2026.25312](https://doi.org/10.18553/jmcp.2026.25312)) model large revenue losses. |
    41	
    42	**List-price cuts: well documented in grey literature, not in academic work.** NCPA's alert (dated 2025-12-10, [page](https://ncpa.org/wac-decreases-coming-take-action-now)) listed the coming WAC decreases: **Imbruvica 2025-12-22**; Eliquis, Farxiga, Jardiance, Glyxambi, Synjardy, Synjardy XR, Trijardy XR, Fiasp, Tresiba and Linzess on 2026-01-01; Lilly's Humalog U-200 and Lyumjev on 2026-02-01. 46brooklyn's January 2026 price review (Elsevier Gold Standard WAC data) reports Eliquis −43.0% and Jardiance −44.4%, the same figures as our NADAC rows. Drug Channels (2026-04-08) counts list-price cuts on **6 of the 10** products with a 2026 MFP. Explanations offered:
    43	
    44	- Under CMS's default refund (WAC minus MFP), a lower WAC shrinks refunds owed and the pharmacy float (Drug Channels, Milliman). Drug Channels warns this also erases any pharmacy margin on MFP drugs.
    45	- Bristol Myers Squibb told Reuters the Eliquis cut avoids Medicare inflation penalties (secondary report, not reopened here).
    46	- The cuts raised 340B prices from near zero (Penn State rural health office: Eliquis $245, Jardiance $120.05 from 2026-07-01; not reopened here).
    47	- For insulin and some inhalers, the 2024 removal of the Medicaid rebate cap is the documented driver (KFF 2024; Levy et al. 2024, JAMA Health Forum, [10.1001/jamahealthforum.2024.3624](https://doi.org/10.1001/jamahealthforum.2024.3624), on Flovent; Han et al. 2026, Health Affairs, [10.1377/hlthaff.2025.01309](https://doi.org/10.1377/hlthaff.2025.01309): generic share rose 23 points after the 2024 inhaler cuts).
    48	
    49	## 4. Strategic responses and innovation
    50	
    51	- **Launch prices.** Shanmugam et al. 2026, JAMA ([10.1001/jama.2026.17742](https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2026.17742)) (press): no measurable change in the launch-price trend after the IRA.
    52	- **Trials and the "pill penalty".** Studies funded by the National Pharmaceutical Council (an industry body) report 25–68% drops in small-molecule investment or trials; Kang et al. 2026, Health Affairs ([10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00720](https://doi.org/10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00720)) find overall trial activity stable, with fewer trials only at manufacturers with negotiated drugs. Wouters et al. 2024, JAMA ([10.1001/jama.2024.16911](https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2024.16911)) find little evidence that biologics need the longer protection. Funding sources matter here and are recorded in [academic.md](ira_notes/academic.md).
    53	- **Deselection.** Beinfeld et al. 2025 model the overlap of Stelara's MFP and biosimilars; no one has measured it.
    54	
    55	## 5. How others have used our data sources
    56	
    57	| Source | Typical uses (examples in [data_uses.md](ira_notes/data_uses.md)) | Lessons for us |
    58	|---|---|---|
    59	| NADAC | Generic markups and PBM spread pricing (Medicaid, Part D), Cost Plus comparisons, generic price spikes; the closest IRA use is Three Axis Advisors for NCPA (January 2025), a pre-launch pharmacy cash-flow model built on NADAC, WAC and the Part D dashboard | Brand NADAC sits about 1% below WAC on average (Levy et al. 2017), so our NADAC/MFP ratio is in practice a list-to-MFP ratio. NADAC excludes specialty pharmacies (Imbruvica and Stelara were missing in Three Axis's data too). Screen for level shifts from the 2024 chain-reporting change. |
    60	| Part D Spending by Drug | Top-spending drugs, price growth per dosage unit, predicting which drugs get selected, savings simulations | Per-claim measures mix price with days supply; per-unit or 30-day-equivalent measures are standard. Use per-unit once the annual 2026 file exists. |
    61	| MFP file | MFP vs list, net (SSR Health), VA and international prices (Wouters 2024; Gabriel 2026; KFF/Peterson: MFPs 2.8 times an 11-country average) | Our NDC-level join to NADAC by date is not in the literature. |
    62	| SDUD and plan files | Medicaid insulin spending after list cuts; difference-in-differences around price events (Wing et al. 2026, NBER); Part D plan pricing files (SPUF) with NADAC (Hernandez et al. 2023; Dusetzina 2024) | The SPUF plan pricing file would show directly whether plans price the 10 drugs at the MFP. |
    63	
    64	## 6. Where our three findings stand
    65	
    66	| Our finding | Status in the literature | Change to make |
    67	|---|---|---|
    68	| 1. Part D gross spending per claim −64% (median) for the 10 drugs, comparison groups flat | **New.** No published measure of realized 2026 spending. | Lead with "gross"; set it beside MedPAC's 45% rebate share and Jeon et al.'s −8.8% projected net change. |
    69	| 2. List-price cuts on 2026-01-01, same-day cuts on related brands | **Confirmed independently** by NCPA (dates) and 46brooklyn (Eliquis −43.0%, Jardiance −44.4%). Not studied academically. | Add Imbruvica's 2025-12-22 cut (no NADAC, so our data missed it). With Januvia (2025) and NovoLog (2024) that is consistent with Drug Channels' count of list-price cuts on 6 of the 10 MFP products. Cite the refund-mechanism, inflation-penalty and 340B explanations as candidates. Report a spending-weighted share as well as the NDC share. |
    70	| 3. NADAC 1.3–3.6 times the MFP for most drugs | **New as a public-data measure.** Grey literature documents refund timing (CMS: 21 days on average) and pharmacy strain; one modelling paper for long-term-care pharmacies. | Frame it as a list-to-MFP benchmark gap, combined with CMS's 21-day refund timing, rather than a measured loss. |
    71	
    72	## 7. The Latin America angle
    73	
    74	- RAND for ASPE (2024, [10.7249/rhq-11-03-05](https://doi.org/10.7249/rhq-11-03-05), 2022 IQVIA MIDAS data) gives US prices as a share of Colombian, Mexican and Chilean prices: for Colombia, 267% overall, about 1,165% for brand originators and 48.5% for unbranded generics (Mexico 172/402/51, Chile 260/748/76, from Table B.1 as transcribed by the scan; worth reopening before quoting).
    75	- No study compares the MFPs with Latin American prices.
    76	- Colombia's Circular 03 de 2013 lists the United States among the 17 international reference countries; whether later circulars changed the basket, and which US price enters it, was not checked. If US list prices enter the reference, the January 2026 cuts could feed into Colombian price ceilings.
    77	- Moye-Holz & Vogler 2021 ([10.1007/s40258-021-00670-4](https://doi.org/10.1007/s40258-021-00670-4)) give the PPP-per-DDD method for Latin American procurement prices.
    78	
    79	## 8. Next steps the literature suggests for our study
    80	
    81	1. Read the plan pricing files (SPUF 2026) to check whether plans price the 10 drugs at the MFP (Hernandez 2023, Dusetzina 2024 method).
    82	2. Add a spending-weighted version of the WAC-cut share (46brooklyn weights by prior-year Medicaid spending from SDUD).
    83	3. Bracket net with published estimates rather than guessing (MedPAC rebate shares, Wouters, Jeon); add VA Big Four prices as a public lower bracket.
    84	4. Write down expected 2027 changes for the 15 IPAY 2027 drugs now, with Gabriel and Li as benchmarks (the "write the outcomes down first" step in our findings).
    85	5. Track the four deselected drugs after 2026-12-31 (Entresto and Stelara generics/biosimilars in NADAC and Part D).
    86	6. Optional Latin America extension: MFP and NADAC against SISMED (Colombia) and CMED (Brazil) prices per DDD.
    87	
    88	## Not used or not confirmed
    89	
    90	Numbers seen only in summaries or press (Castle 2026, Shanmugam 2026, the DLA Piper survey, the NCPA survey percentages and sample size, Reuters on Bristol Myers Squibb, the Penn State 340B prices, the CBO $8.8 billion figure via CRFB/STAT) are marked or left out above. Several publisher and government sites (cbo.gov, congress.gov, healthaffairs.org) refused scripted access. The scans' own "Not verified" lists are in the three notes files.
     1	# Medicare drug price negotiation, first year: what moved in January 2026
     2	
     3	Status: draft, 2026-10-20. Descriptive analysis, triple-checked: Codex (GPT-6 Astra) replicated every number blind (PASS WITH FIXES, `reviews/pharma_ira_codex_astra/report.md`) and Agy (Gemini 4 Argon) recomputed blind and reviewed the method (PASS WITH FIXES, `reviews/pharma_ira_agy_argon/REPORT.md`). Both reviews' fixes are applied, and a Codex follow-up confirmed the revised numbers (8,542 comparisons, `reviews/pharma_ira_codex_astra/followup/report.md`). Numbers come from `pharma/scripts/ira_analysis.py`; tables in [outputs/ira/summary.md](outputs/ira/summary.md).
     4	
     5	## Question
     6	
     7	The first ten Maximum Fair Prices (MFPs) negotiated under the Inflation Reduction Act took effect on 2026-01-01 for Medicare Part D. Three public CMS sources show what changed, from three angles:
     8	
     9	| Angle | Source | Measure |
    10	|---|---|---|
    11	| What Part D records as spent | Quarterly Part D Spending by Drug (2025 full year; 2026 Q1, preliminary) | Gross spending per claim |
    12	| What manufacturers list | NADAC Comparison rows whose reason is a WAC adjustment | Early-year WAC-related NADAC cuts and increases, by NDC |
    13	| What pharmacies pay on invoice | NADAC weekly files 2024–2026, joined by NDC to the CMS MFP file | NADAC per unit divided by the MFP per unit in effect |
    14	
    15	Comparison groups: the 15 drugs negotiated for 2027 (MFP from 2027-01-01), the 15 selected for negotiation for 2028 (prices not yet published), and other brand-name products.
    16	
    17	## Findings
    18	
    19	**1. Part D gross spending per claim fell by at least 30% for all ten negotiated drugs (median −64%), while the comparison groups barely moved.** Between 2025 and 2026 Q1 the changes ranged from −30% for the NovoLog entry to −71% for Enbrel. The NovoLog entry pools two brands: named NovoLog products fell 9% (their list price was already cut in 2024) and Fiasp 72%. Holding each group's 2025 drug mix fixed, the change was −61% for the ten, +1.5% for drugs negotiated for 2027, +1.2% for those selected for 2028 and +1.1% for 741 other brand-name products (their median was +0.9%, middle half −2.0% to +3.6%). Only 2% of other brands (13 products) fell by 30% or more; 11 of the 13 are products with WAC-related cuts at the same time: Novo Nordisk's Tresiba, Eli Lilly's Humalog U-200 and Lyumjev, and Boehringer Ingelheim's empagliflozin combinations (Synjardy, Glyxambi, Trijardy XR). In the 2027 group, Linzess (−49%) also follows a 50% list-price cut on 2026-01-01.
    20	
    21	This is what the payment rules would produce: for a selected drug, the law caps the Part D negotiated price at the MFP plus dispensing fees ([SSA §1860D-2(d)(1)(D), added by Pub. L. 117-169](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-117publ169/html/PLAW-117publ169.htm)), and gross drug cost in this file is built from those claim amounts. The aggregate data are consistent with MFP-based payment; they do not prove it for every claim or separate it from other changes.
    22	
    23	![Part D spending per claim](outputs/ira/chart_partd_per_claim.png)
    24	
    25	**2. On the day the MFPs began, four of the ten negotiated drugs took large list-price cuts, and so did some related non-selected brands.** NADAC records WAC-based cuts effective 2026-01-01 of 43% for Eliquis, 44% for Jardiance, 37% for Farxiga and 75% for Fiasp (the CMS NovoLog entry covers NovoLog and Fiasp; NovoLog itself had been cut 75% on 2024-01-01). Januvia, and Janumet in the 2027 group, had been cut 42% on 2025-01-01. In January 2026, 30% of the brand NDCs of the 2026 drugs had a cut of 20% or more, against 1.5% of other brand NDCs. Most of those other-brand cuts are not independent: 30 of the 33 are Boehringer Ingelheim's empagliflozin combinations, cut by the same 44.4% on the same day as Jardiance, and three are Tresiba; through February, Lilly's 70% cuts on Lyumjev and Humalog U-200 (2026-02-01) join them. List-price increases were as common for the 2026 drugs as for other brands once February is included (46% of their NDCs, from Xarelto on 2026-02-03 and Enbrel, Entresto and Stelara in January, against 39%). Imbruvica, which has no NADAC, also cut its list price on 2025-12-22 according to NCPA's notice of WAC decreases, so six of the ten have had list-price cuts since 2024. These are NADAC changes that CMS attributes to WAC changes, not a separate WAC series; 46brooklyn's independent WAC data show the same Eliquis (−43.0%) and Jardiance (−44.4%) cuts. Insulin list-price cuts also cluster around the removal of the Medicaid rebate cap on 2024-01-01, so the MFP is one of several candidate explanations; these data cannot separate them.
    26	
    27	**3. NADAC remains well above the MFP for most of these drugs.** For brand-classified NDCs, NADAC, the average invoice cost of retail community pharmacies, was 2.2 to 4.0 times the 2026 MFP per unit at the end of December 2025, except NovoLog, already at 1.0 after its 2024 cut. By October 2026, after the January list-price cuts, it was 0.99 times for Fiasp, 1.33 for Eliquis, 1.65 for Jardiance and 2.00 for Farxiga. Januvia stayed at 2.70, and Xarelto (2.84), Entresto (2.22), Stelara (3.32) and Enbrel (3.57) rose slightly, by 2% to 5%, with their 2026 list-price increases. Ratios use the MFP in effect on each snapshot date (CMS corrected the Entresto Sprinkle MFP from 2026-06-01) and agree at the displayed precision on NDCs present in both snapshots. The CMS list also covers unbranded and authorized-generic NDCs (Novo Nordisk's insulin aspart, dapagliflozin) that carry generic NADAC rates and their own MFPs; they are reported separately in the summary tables.
    28	
    29	This is a gap between an invoice benchmark and the MFP. CMS lets manufacturers effectuate the MFP prospectively or through retrospective refunds, by default computed as WAC minus MFP, with the Medicare Transaction Facilitator exchanging claim data ([CMS final guidance, sections 40.4 to 40.4.4](https://www.cms.gov/files/document/ipay-2028-final-guidance.pdf)). NADAC records invoice prices for purchases across all payers, so a refund paid outside the invoice does not change it directly; brand NADAC moves with list-price changes, monthly surveys and help-desk reviews ([NADAC methodology](https://www.medicaid.gov/medicaid-chip-program-information/by-topics/prescription-drugs/ful-nadac-downloads/nadacmethodology.pdf)). NADAC minus MFP is a benchmark gap, not a measured pharmacy receivable; the data here do not show actual pharmacy losses, refund timing or amounts. No NADAC observations matched the Imbruvica NDCs.
    30	
    31	![NADAC relative to MFP](outputs/ira/chart_nadac_vs_mfp.png)
    32	
    33	## What others have found (context)
    34	
    35	A literature and grey-literature scan (2026-10-20, [literature/ira_negotiation_literature_review.md](../literature/ira_negotiation_literature_review.md)) found no published measure of realized 2026 spending or prices for these drugs; findings 1 and 3 appear to be new, and finding 2 is documented only in grey literature (NCPA, 46brooklyn, Drug Channels). Two numbers frame finding 1: MedPAC reports that rebates cut gross Part D costs by nearly 45% for the 2026 drugs in 2024, and Jeon et al. (Value in Health, 2026) project only an 8.8% fall in 2026 net spending on them. Much of the gross drop is the list price falling toward the old net price. For finding 3, CMS reports that MFP refunds reach pharmacies within 21 days of the date of service, on average.
    36	
    37	## How to read this (limits)
    38	
    39	- **Gross, not net. These data do not estimate net savings.** Part D spending is gross drug cost before manufacturer rebates (it includes dispensing fees and pools all strengths of a brand), and these brands carried confidential rebates before 2026; drug-level net spending is not in this dataset. For context only, CMS projected 22% aggregate net savings for the first ten drugs against a 2023 counterfactual ([CMS fact sheet](https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/medicare-drug-price-negotiation-program-negotiated-prices-initial-price-applicability-year-2026)); that is not an observed 2026 figure.
    40	- **Preliminary quarter.** 2026 Q1 Part D data were extracted three months after the quarter; CMS calls them preliminary and warns they can be revised.
    41	- **Mix, not only price.** Spending per claim also moves with days supply, strength and form. Entresto (brand claims down 87% on an annualized run-rate after generic entry in 2025), Stelara (down 44%, after biosimilar launches in 2025) and Farxiga (down 30%) may reflect mix and substitution as well as price; this analysis does not decompose it.
    42	- **Drug mix across products.** Pooled per-claim spending for other brands rose 10.9%, but with 2025 claim shares held fixed it rose 1.1%: the difference is a shift of claims toward costlier drugs (GLP-1s, specialty drugs), not a price rise. The fixed-weight figures in finding 1 remove that shift; seasonality between a full year and a first quarter is not tested.
    43	- **Name-based groups.** Part D rows have brand names, not NDCs. Selected families use the brand names in the CMS file (NovoLog Mix 70/30 is excluded from the NovoLog family because its NDCs are not on the list, and stays in other brands). Rows whose ingredients exactly match a selected drug but carry no selected brand name (unbranded and authorized generics) are left out of every group. "Other brands" is a name-based classification (brand string differs from the generic, its first word is not in the generic name, supplies excluded) and still contains a few generic products.
    44	- **NADAC is retail, per unit and set per rate group.** It covers retail community pharmacies only; brand rates follow WAC changes between surveys. Ratios are unweighted medians across brand NDCs (69 in December 2025, 70 in October 2026), not weighted by claims. NADAC and CMS both use EA/ML/GM billing units, and per-NDC ratios were checked for outliers (two were found: an expired Entresto Sprinkle MFP, now handled by date, and the Fiasp PumpCart, which carries the vial MFP in the CMS file).
    45	- **The 2026 list is already changing.** The CMS file end-dates the MFP of Entresto, NovoLog/Fiasp, Stelara and Xarelto at 2026-12-31 (marked "deselected"), so from 2027 only six of the ten keep an MFP.
    46	- **Not causal.** A before/after change against comparison groups describes what happened. It does not separate the MFP from list-price strategy, the 2024 Medicaid rebate-cap change, generic and biosimilar entry, the 2025 Part D redesign, rebate renegotiation or anticipation by drugs selected later (Janumet and Linzess cut list prices before their MFPs). Patients' out-of-pocket costs are not measured.
    47	
    48	## Where this could go
    49	
    50	1. Add 2026 Q2 when CMS releases it, and earlier first quarters from archived quarterly releases to test seasonality.
    51	2. Spending per dosage unit from the annual Part D file (available for 2026 once released) removes days-supply variation, though strengths and forms still need standardizing.
    52	3. The 2027 group: write down the outcomes for the next 15 drugs, whose MFPs start on 2027-01-01, before their data arrive (the 2026 results have already been seen).
    53	4. Pharmacy exposure: an estimate of what pharmacies front before refunds needs eligible quantities, actual acquisition and refund dates and 340B handling, not only NADAC and MFP per unit.

